  "content": {
    "id": "p74-q22-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "22",
    "prompt": "Find the gradient of the tangent to $y=\\sqrt{4-x^2}$ at $x=2$. Explain your answer.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "The gradient is undefined: the tangent at $(2,0)$ is the vertical line $x=2$.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}y&=(4-x^2)^{1/2}\\\\y'&=\\frac12(4-x^2)^{-1/2}(-2x)\\\\y'&=-\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4-x^2}}\\end{align*}$$\nAt $x=2$, the denominator is zero, so the gradient is undefined. The curve is the upper semicircle $x^2+y^2=4$, whose tangent at $(2,0)$ is the vertical line $x=2$.\n\nhttps://www.desmos.com/calculator/bwo9dgalgd",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 30
  },
